🏥 Heart Failure Prediction System
This project is an MLOps-ready web application that predicts the risk of heart failure based on clinical patient data. It uses a Random Forest Classifier to analyze patterns and a StandardScaler to ensure data consistency.

🧠 How it Works: The Chef & The Translator
To understand this system, imagine a Professional Chef and a Global Food Supplier:

The Scaler (The Translator): The Chef only understands measurements in "standard units." However, the Supplier (the Patient) provides data in various units—some in large magnitudes (like Platelets), others in small units (like Age). Before the Chef touches the ingredients, the Scaler translates everything into the exact standardized units the Chef expects.

The Model (The Chef): The Chef has been trained in a lab to recognize patterns in these standardized ingredients. Once the Scaler hands over the translated data, the Chef predicts the outcome: High Risk or Low Risk.

MLOps Expert Note: You can never have the Chef without the Translator. If you give the Chef raw data without scaling it first, the "dish" (prediction) will be ruined because the model was specifically trained on scaled data.

🚀 Project Overview
Goal: Predict the DEATH_EVENT (High Risk vs. Low Risk) based on 12 clinical features.

Model: Random Forest with 100 estimators and class_weight='balanced' to handle data imbalance.

Deployment: Built with Streamlit for real-time interaction.

📂 Repository Structure
app.py: The Streamlit web application code.

code.ipynb: The Jupyter Notebook containing the data analysis and training logic.

heart_disease_model.pkl: The trained Random Forest "Chef" (Model Artifact).

scaler.pkl: The StandardScaler "Translator" (Preprocessing Artifact).

requirements.txt: List of dependencies required for deployment.

💻 Local Environment Setup (Anaconda)
To recreate the "Kitchen" used for training, follow these steps to set up an isolated environment:

Create the Environment:

Bash
conda create --name heart_failure_env python=3.12 -y
Activate the Environment:

Bash
conda activate heart_failure_env
Install Dependencies:

Bash
pip install pandas numpy matplotlib seaborn scikit-learn streamlit joblib ipykernel
Register the Kernel (For Jupyter):

Bash
python -m ipykernel install --user --name heart_failure_env --display-name "Python (Heart-ML)"
🛠️ Installation & Usage
Clone the repository:

Bash
git clone https://github.com/CarlosJoseChaconChavarria/project01_heart_disease.git
Install dependencies:

Bash
pip install -r requirements.txt
Run the application locally:

Bash
streamlit run app.py
The app will be available at localhost:8501.

🌐 Deployment (Streamlit Cloud)
The final stage of this MLOps pipeline is deploying the local application to the cloud for public access:

GitHub Connection: Code and artifacts are pushed to a public GitHub repository.

Streamlit Cloud Setup: Log in to Streamlit Cloud and select "Deploy a public app from GitHub".

Launch: Point the repository to CarlosJoseChaconChavarria/project01_heart_disease and the main file to app.py.

🧠 MLOps Concepts Implemented
Artifact Versioning: Saving the model and scaler as inseparable pairs to ensure production matches training.

Feature Scaling: Using standardization to prevent high-magnitude features like platelets from overpowering features like age.

Reproducibility: Using random_state=42 across all data splits and model training.

Decoupling: The dataset (.csv) is removed from the production repo as the app only requires the pre-trained artifacts to function.
